s/ui/* for consistency.\\\\\\\\n• Use CSS Variables for theming; ready for dark-mode toggle.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Navigation & Routing Map\\\\\\\\n/\\\\\\\\n├── contact\\\\\\\\n├── bylaws\\\\\\\\n├── schools\\\\\\\\n└── member\\\\\\\\n    ├── dues\\\\\\\\n    ├── meetings\\\\\\\\n    └── news-events\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Accessibility & UX Guarantees\\\\\\\\n• All interactive elements reachable by keyboard; focus outline visible.\\\\\\\\n• aria-labels on icons & form inputs; color contrast ≥ 4.5:1.\\\\\\\\n• prefers-reduced-motion respected for animations.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Responsive Breakpoints Preview (representative)\\\\\\\\n• Mobile (<640 px): stacked layout, hamburger nav.\\\\\\\\n• Tablet (641-1023 px): grid-2 columns, collapsible sidebar.\\\\\\\\n• Desktop (≥1024 px): full multi-column, sticky TOC.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Integration Notes for Ops Team\\\\\\\\n• Next.js front can be deployed on Vercel (edge-optimized) or containerized w/ Docker; environment vars NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_KEY etc. tie into backend endpoints.\\\\\\\\n• Auth middleware expects backend route /api/auth/refresh to validate and refresh JWT.\\\\\\\\n• SWR’s fetcher points to (process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL + endpoint).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Estimated File/Component Breakdown (organised by feature folder)\\\\\\\\n  /components\\\\\\\\n    /ui (Button.tsx, Card.tsx, Input.tsx, Modal.tsx, etc.)\\\\\\\\n  /layouts/MainLayout.tsx\\\\\\\\n  /pages\\\\\\\\n    contact.tsx\\\\\\\\n    bylaws.tsx\\\\\\\\n    schools.tsx\\\\\\\\n    /member/dues.tsx\\\\\\\\n    /member/meetings.tsx\\\\\\\\n    /member/news-events.tsx\\\\\\\\n  /lib (auth.ts, fetcher.ts)\\\\\\\\n  /styles (globals.css, tailwind.css)\\\\\\\\n  /tests (unit & e2e)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n11. Next Steps / Suggestions\\\\\\\\n• Obtain logo/brand assets & any brand guidelines.\\\\\\\\n• Confirm backend auth + payment provider selections.\\\\\\\\n• Decide if server components (Next 13/14) or classic pages.\\\\\\\\n• Finalize content (bylaws text, school list, meeting PDFs) to wire into CMS or markdown repo.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of Report.\\\\\\\\n──────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T18:32:44.801Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The Directors have returned with comprehensive and well-structured plans. This is excellent work. I will now synthesize their reports, resolve the minor overlap in responsibilities, and issue clear directives to move the project forward.\\n\\nMy primary executive decision is to align with the Operations plan for content management. Using a Headless CMS like Sanity.io is the best solution for the user's requirement of having a non-technical person update content. The Backend will therefore focus on user data, authentication, and secure file management.\\n\\nFirst, I will store these excellent plans in the Company File System for our records.\\n\\n###<store-ops-plan-01>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"Director Lagret's Initial Operations & Deployment Plan (OPS-PLAN-01):\\\\n\\\\n=======================\\\\nPROPOSED CORE TECHNOLOGY & DEPLOYMENT PLAN FOR HOA WEBSITE\\\\n=======================\\\\n1. Guiding Principles\\\\n   • Non-technical editor friendliness (single sign-on, rich-text, media upload, preview)\\\\n   • Low monthly cost / generous free tiers\\\\n   • Zero-ops hosting & CI/CD\\\\n   • Fast, secure, SEO-friendly rendering\\\\n   • Scalable without vendor lock-in\\\\n\\\\n2. Chosen Stack (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Happy Path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\n   A. Front-end\\\\n      • Framework: Next.js 14 with the App Router & TypeScript\\\\n      • Rendering: Static Site Generation (SSG) + Incremental Static Regeneration (ISR) for extremely fast pages that auto-update when content changes\\\\n      • Styling: Tailwind CSS + Headless UI\\\\n      • Optional dynamic bits (contact form, search) served via Vercel Serverless Functions\\\\n\\\\n   B. Content Management\\\\n      • Headless CMS: Sanity io (hosted SaaS)\\\\n         – Rich-text “Portable Text” editor, media library, drag-drop ordering\\\\n         – Role-based access & audit trail (Editors vs Admins)\\\\n         – Free tier: 3 users, 100k API requests/mo, 500 MB assets (adequate for HOA scale)\\\\n         – Single Studio codebase checked into repo → developers keep schema in Git; Sanity hosts the DB & API\\\\n      • Schema Blueprint (initial):\\\\n         – newsArticle {title, slug, teaser, body[], publishDate, heroImage}\\\\n         – event {title, slug, description[], startDate, endDate, location, flyerImage}\\\\n         – siteSettings {homepageBanners[], contactEmail, phone}\\\\n\\\\n   C. Hosting & Deployment\\\\n      • Vercel (Hobby → Pro as needed)\\\\n         – Git-connected: push to main triggers build & deploy\\\\n         – Automatic SSL, edge CDN, analytics\\\\n      • Sanity Webhook → Vercel “/api/revalidate” endpoint to trigger ISR once an editor presses “Publish”. Result: content live in ≤60 seconds with no manual deploy.\\\\n      • DNS: Custom domain pointed to Vercel, free SSL certs\\\\n\\\\n3. Workflow for Non-Technical Editors\\\\n   1) Visit https://<org>.sanity.studio (password-protected or Google login)\\\\n   2) Create / edit News or Events with WYSIWYG interface\\\\n   3) Click “Publish”.\\\\n   4) Sanity fires webhook → Vercel; ISR re-renders only affected pages; site instantly updated.\\\\n   No Git/GitHub knowledge required.\\\\n\\\\n4. Cost Projection\\\\n   • Vercel Hobby = $0 (100 GB bandwidth /mo, 1 GB build minutes)\\\\n   • Sanity Free = $0 (see limits above)\\\\n   • Domain ≈ $12/yr (Namecheap / Route 53)\\\\n   • Optional upgrades: Vercel Pro $20/mo if traffic grows; Sanity Growth $15/mo if user count or bandwidth grows.\\\\n\\\\n5. Security & Compliance\\\\n   • HTTPS everywhere (Vercel auto-provisioned certs)\\\\n   • CMS: RBAC; 2FA support\\\\n   • Content requests served via edge CDN, no direct DB exposure\\\\n   • Secrets (webhook tokens, revalidate keys) stored in Vercel & Sanity encrypted env vars\\\\n\\\\n6. Coordination Points for Other Directors\\\\n   • Backend Dir: Define /api/revalidate endpoint signature & any additional APIs (e.g., authenticated resident portal) using Next.js serverless or separate micro-service.\\\\n   • Frontend Dir: Adopt component library & content fetch hooks (Sanity client, or GraphQL if they prefer). Follow the above content schema; we can evolve schemas via migration scripts checked into repo.\\\\n\\\\n7. Alternative (in case requirements change)\\\\n   • Self-hosted Strapi on Render.com – open-source, Node-based, quick spin-up; slightly higher ops cost, but full data ownership.\\\\n   • Git-based Netlify CMS if budget must be $0 and editors comfortable using Git PR previews (less friendly for truly non-technical users, so kept as fallback).\\\\n\\\\n8. Next Steps (if plan approved)\\\\n   1) Initialize mono-repo (frontend + Sanity Studio) on GitHub.\\\\n   2) Configure Vercel project & environment variables.\\\\n   3) Scaffold Sanity Studio with schemas above; invite editors.\\\\n   4) Build News & Events pages with ISR and live preview.\\\\n   5) Document editor guide (1-page PDF / video GIF).\\\"\\n}\\n###<store-ops-plan-01>###\\n###<store-be-plan-01>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"Director Lystra's Initial Backend Architecture Plan (BE-PLAN-01):\\\\n\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n1. Guiding Principles\\\\n• Security-first: encryption in transit & at rest, OWASP Top-10 mitigations, least-privilege RBAC.\\\\n• Modular, test-driven, container-ready, horizontally scalable.\\\\n• Developer-friendly (TypeScript), clear API contract for the frontend team.\\\\n\\\\n2. Chosen Tech Stack (aligned with Ops Director’s preference for JS-centric stack)\\\\n• Runtime  : Node.js 20 + TypeScript\\\\n• Web FW   : Express 4 (with express-async-errors) or Fastify (pluggable, faster) – defaulting to Fastify unless Ops objects.\\\\n• ORM      : Prisma 5 (PostgreSQL adapter) to keep SQL transparent but typed.\\\\n• DB       : PostgreSQL 15 on AWS RDS (or CloudSQL/AzurePG; local via Docker).\\\\n• Auth     : JSON Web Tokens (short-lived Access, long-lived Refresh) + BCrypt-salted hashes; optional social login add-ons later.\\\\n• Queue    : Lightweight BullMQ/Redis for email dispatch so web thread stays cold-path.\\\\n• Email    : AWS SES (or SendGrid) via signed SMTP creds.\\\\n• Container: Docker; Prod orchestrated via ECS Fargate or Kubernetes cluster managed by Ops.\\\\n• Observab.: Pino logger → Loki/Grafana; metrics via Prom-compatible exporter.\\\\n\\\\n3. Database Schema (simplified ER diagram)\\\\n• users\\\\n  – id (PK, UUID)\\\\n  – email (unique, indexed)\\\\n  – password_hash\\\\n  – full_name\\\\n  – unit_number (varchar)   // apt / house identifier\\\\n  – phone (nullable)\\\\n  – is_email_verified (bool)\\\\n  – created_at, updated_at\\\\n\\\\n• roles (seed table)\\\\n  – id (PK)  e.g. RESIDENT, BOARD_MEMBER, ADMIN\\\\n\\\\n• user_roles (user_id FK → users, role_id FK → roles)\\\\n\\\\n• refresh_tokens\\\\n  – id (PK, UUID)\\\\n  – user_id FK\\\\n  – token_hash (sha256)\\\\n  – revoked (bool), expires_at, created_at, created_by_ip\\\\n\\\\n• announcements\\\\n  – id (PK, UUID)\\\\n  – title, body(markdown|html), published_at, author_id FK → users\\\\n\\\\n• documents\\\\n  – id (PK, UUID)\\\\n  – file_path (S3 URL), label, visibility (public | resident | board)\\\\n  – uploaded_by FK → users, uploaded_at\\\\n\\\\n• contact_messages\\\\n  – id (PK, UUID)\\\\n  – name, email, subject, message_text\\\\n  – status (NEW | IN_REVIEW | CLOSED)\\\\n  – received_at, handled_by FK → users\\\\n\\\\n(Prisma schema & migration scripts will be generated.)\\\\n\\\\n4. API Design (REST, versioned under /api/v1)\\\\n─ Auth\\\\n  • POST  /auth/register             → create resident account, send verify-email job\\\\n  • POST  /auth/login                → returns {access, refresh}\\\\n  • POST  /auth/refresh              → rotates refresh token\\\\n  • POST  /auth/logout               → revoke refresh token\\\\n  • POST  /auth/forgot-password\\\\\\\\n  • POST  /auth/reset-password\\\\\\\\n\\\\n─ Users (protected, role-based)\\\\n  • GET   /users/me                  → current profile\\\\n  • PATCH /users/me                  → update profile fields\\\\n  • GET   /users/:id                 → ADMIN/BOARD only\\\\n  • GET   /users?role=BOARD_MEMBER   → list board members\\\\n\\\\n─ Announcements\\\\n  • GET   /announcements             → public/resident filtered by role\\\\n  • POST  /announcements             → BOARD/ADMIN (create)\\\\n  • PATCH /announcements/:id         → author or ADMIN\\\\n  • DELETE/announcements/:id         → ADMIN\\\\n\\\\n─ Documents\\\\n  • GET   /documents?visibility=...  → returns presigned S3 URLs\\\\n  • POST  /documents (multipart)     → BOARD/ADMIN (uploads via signed URL flow)\\\\n\\\\n─ Contact Us\\\\n  • POST  /contact                   → anonymous or authenticated; body {name,email,subject,message}\\\\\\\\n  • GET   /contact/:id               → ADMIN only to view & change status\\\\n\\\\nAll endpoints follow RFC 7807 error object, 429 for rate-limit, express-validator for payloads.\\\\n\\\\n5. Authentication & Security Controls\\\\n• Password: 12+ chars, complexity rules, bcrypt-12 rounds.\\\\n• JWT Access: 15 min exp, signed w/ RS256 private key; kid rotation.\\\\n• Refresh token: 30 d lifetime, stored hashed in DB; rotate on every use (token theft detection).\\\\n• Email-verification & password-reset tokens stored hashed, expire 24 h.\\\\n• Middleware stack: helmet(), cors(allowedOrigins env), rateLimiter (redis-backing), csrf() for cookie-based flows.\\\\n• RBAC Guard: decorator or middleware mapping route → roles.\\\\n• Secrets via AWS Secrets Manager / Kubernetes Secrets; never baked into image.\\\\n• Automated SCA & SAST in CI (GitHub Actions).\\\\n\\\\n6. Contact Us Logic Flow\\\\n1. Client calls POST /contact.\\\\n2. Server validates & rate-limits (max 5/hr/IP).\\\\n3. Persist contact_messages row (status=NEW).\\\\n4. Queue job ‘email:contact’ with message ID.\\\\n5. Worker pulls job → formats email → SES send to designated board address.\\\\n6. Board/ops can view & mark status via ADMIN dashboard API.\\\\n\\\\n7. Testing Strategy\\\\n• Unit tests: Jest + supertest (service & controller layers)\\\\n• Integration: testcontainers spinning PostgreSQL & Redis.\\\\n• E2E smoke: simple k6 script hitting staging endpoints.\\\\n\\\\n8. Deployment Pipeline Sketch\\\\nGitHub → Actions\\\\n  – install, lint, test\\\\n  – build Docker image\\\\n  – push to ECR / GHCR\\\\n  – trigger staging deploy (Terraform plan-apply or Helm upgrade)\\\\n  – seed migrations via prisma migrate\\\\n  – promote to prod via manual approval.\\\\n\\\\n9. Work Breakdown (next steps)\\\\nA. Recruit Supervisor-level leads:\\\\n   • API/Service Lead – owns Fastify services & tests.\\\\n   • Auth Lead – builds the JWT/Refresh & RBAC modules.\\\\n   • DB Lead – finalizes Prisma schema & migrations.\\\\n   • Ops Integration Lead – scripts CI/CD & Terraform hand-off with Ops Director.\\\\nB. Each lead will spawn Attendants for individual files/components.\\\\nC. Produce detailed DEP specifying container build, secrets injection, environment matrix.\\\\nD. Submit DEP to Partagaz D for review, then request phase shift to Execution.\\\\n\\\\n10. Open Items / Alignment Needed\\\\n• Confirm Fastify vs Express preference.\\\\n• Domain for email (SES verified?) and SMTP provider selection.\\\\n• Whether social login (Google) is desired MVP.\\\\n• Logging/monitoring stack preference (ELK vs Loki).\\\"\\n}\\n###<store-be-plan-01>###\\n###<store-fe-plan-01>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"Director Jung's Initial Frontend Plan (FE-PLAN-01):\\\\n\\\\n──────────────────────────────────\\\\n1. Guiding Principles\\\\n• Simplicity & Clarity: Clean layouts, limit